Patch 6019: the NP identity getters and port_send's EBUSY log once and on change, and no NP line prints an online id or address.

J-Stars Victory VS+ calls sceNpGetNpId about 15 times a second while it is online, and sceNpGetNpId printed the player's
online id on every call: tens of thousands of lines, most of a session's log, the start of the log pushed out of a
support bundle's size budget and the online id written into it over and over. sys_event_port_send printed
"queue N FULL -> EBUSY" on every EBUSY: over a hundred thousand lines in one session. The EBUSY itself is right (a size-1
queue that is still full refuses the next event, as on the console, and the title sends again later); only the logging was.

- sceNp.c: GetNpId, GetOnlineId, GetOnlineName and GetUserProfile (and the sceNpManager* forms, which call them) each log
  their first call and then only a call whose answer differs from the last one logged, with the call count. The line
  says "online id set (N chars)" or "online id changed (N chars)"; the id is never printed. sceNpInit logs the id's
  length instead of the id. What the getters write is unchanged.
- sys_event.c, new evt_full_log.h: each port logs its first EBUSY, the end of its first run of EBUSY (the next event
  its queue takes) with the run's length, and at exit, if it ever answered EBUSY, the total, the number of runs and the
  longest run. At most three lines per port. What the send returns is unchanged.
- sceNpMatching2.c, sceNpSignaling.c, np_psnr.c: other players are named by their member, connection or psnr user
  number, never by online id or address. The psnr lines no longer print this player's online id, public address or
  the server's name (the port stays; the launcher logs the server it was given). An invalid online ID is reported
  with where it came from (--username or PS3_NP_ONLINE_ID) and its length.
- sceNpBasic.c: SendMessage, SendMessageAttachment, SendInGameInvitation and AddBlockListEntry log the other player's online
  id by its length only, and the attachment line says whether there is a subject, not what it says.
Logging only. Ours.
Tests: libs/network/tests/test_np_identity_log.c and runtime/syscalls/tests/test_evt_full_log.c, plus a source check
of the NP files' printf arguments (installer/tools/np_log_test.sh).

